counterexample:
-- The queries are equivalent under the given constraints, so no INSERT statements can produce a counterexample.
-- For instance, the following legal test data produces the same output for both queries:

INSERT INTO table2 (c10, c3) VALUES (1, 11);
INSERT INTO table2 (c10, c3) VALUES (2, 10);

INSERT INTO table4 (c15, c14, c10) VALUES (100, 'alpha', 1);
INSERT INTO table4 (c15, c14, c10) VALUES (101, 'beta', 2);

-- Q1 will return 'alpha' (because only the join on c10=1 meets the condition) and
-- Q2 will also return 'alpha'.
-- Thus, under the given schema no counterexample exists that yields different results.

