counterexample:
-- There is no valid counterexample since the queries are equivalent.
-- However, here is an example set of INSERT statements (test data) that shows they compute the same result:

INSERT INTO table1 (c7, c6) VALUES (1, 'EUR');
INSERT INTO table1 (c7, c6) VALUES (2, 'USD');

INSERT INTO table4 (c15, c8, c14, c7) VALUES (10, '2012-08-26', '12:30:00', 1);
INSERT INTO table4 (c15, c8, c14, c7) VALUES (20, '2012-08-26', '13:30:00', 1);
INSERT INTO table4 (c15, c8, c14, c7) VALUES (30, '2012-08-26', '12:00:00', 2);

-- Running Q1 or Q2 on the above data:
-- The only row satisfying (T1.c8 = '2012-08-26', T1.c14 < '13:00:00' and T2.c6 = 'EUR' with the join T1.c7 = T2.c7) is the first row (c15 = 10), 
-- so both queries return a count of 1.
-- This illustrates that even with differing data, the queries will always return the same result under the given constraints.

